        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Dhokla"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " is a Gujarati classic: "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "dhokla term ",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "dhokla-term"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ". This recipe uses "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "eno",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "eno"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " as a fast-rising agent instead of overnight fermentation. The "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "tempering oil",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "glossaryTooltip",
                "attrs": {
                  "termSlug": "tempering-oil"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " is poured while the dhokla is still hot so it absorbs into the surface."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Mix the eno into the batter at the last moment and pour into the steaming tin immediately. Do not let the batter sit after adding the leavening."
          }
        ]
      },
